    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d555">

                <pb facs="0522" n="512" xml:id="pg512"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/14748: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-24T19:00:00+09:00">June 24, 1941—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 24—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>874. Embassy’s 872, June 24, 4 p.m. Wang-Konoye statement. It is believed that
                    the statement concerning mutual defense against communism were inserted at
                    Wang’s insistence, as he was perturbed by Japan’s apparent <hi
                        rendition="#italic">rapprochement</hi> with the Soviet Union. As expected,
                    the Japanese Government promised to assist toward extending the authority of the
                    Nanking régime, but specific plans were not mentioned despite a suggestion that
                    their promising would result in winning the confidence of the people.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department via Shanghai. Shanghai please repeat to Chungking, Nanking
                    and Peiping.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
